\usepackage[a4paper,margin=26mm]{geometry}
\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,hyperref}
\setlength{\emergencystretch}{2em}
\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}
\title{Four vertices outside a Moore edge tree}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
We classify the connected graphs of order thirty, degree four and girth
six having twenty-one six-cycles through each edge. The acquired lower
bound already excludes smaller orders. The remaining classification has
a short local reduction and a complete finite branching certificate.

\section{The exterior graph and its incidences}
Fix an edge $uv$. The acquired Moore edge tree has twenty-six vertices:
two rooted trees of depth two, with level sizes $1,3,9$, joined at their
roots. Let $A,B$ be the opposite sets of nine leaves. Each is divided
into three branches of three leaves. All internal vertices have their
final degrees. Any further edge among tree vertices joins $A$ to $B$;
other choices would create a cycle of length at most five.
Let $Y$ be the remaining four vertices.

\begin{lemma}
There are twenty-one edges between $A$ and $B$, six edges from each
of $A,B$ to $Y$, and two edges induced by $Y$. Thus the exterior graph
is either two disjoint edges, or a path of two edges and an isolated vertex.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
An edge between $A$ and $B$ closes a unique six-cycle through $uv$.
Every six-cycle through $uv$ arises this way, so there are twenty-one
such edges. Each leaf set has twenty-seven degree incidences left after
its parent edges. Six incidences on each side therefore lead to $Y$.
The degree sum on $Y$ is sixteen; twelve incidences leave it, and the
remaining four give its two internal edges. The two listed simple graphs
are precisely the graphs with four vertices and two edges.
\end{proof}

A vertex of $Y$ meets at most one leaf in each branch, since otherwise
it creates a quadrangle. Two adjacent vertices of $Y$ cannot both meet
the same branch. A shared leaf would give a triangle; distinct leaves
would give a pentagon through their common parent. Vertices at distance
two within $Y$ cannot share a leaf, since that gives a quadrangle.
Finally, any two vertices of $Y$ share at most one leaf in the whole graph.

Here is an explicit enumeration of all remaining local forms. Label the
exterior vertices $0,1,2,3$. Use exterior edges $01,23$ in the first case
and $01,12$ in the second. For a branch, choose a subset of the exterior
vertices meeting it, then partition that subset into at most three blocks;
a block consists of the exterior neighbours of one leaf. Require that the
chosen subset contains no adjacent exterior pair, and that no block
contains a pair at exterior distance two. Empty leaves fill the remaining
positions. Sorting the blocks merely chooses the names of the three leaves.

For each side choose a multiset of three branch forms, thereby choosing
the names of its three parents. Require six exterior incidences on each
side. The total number of incidences of an exterior vertex $p$ must be
$4-\deg_Y(p)$, and no exterior pair may occur together in two blocks.
Construct each resulting literal graph and reject it if its girth is
less than six. These are necessary conditions already proved above.
Every possible graph remains represented: label its exterior graph in
one of the two listed ways, then permute its branches and leaves into the
specified orders. No other graph identification is performed.

\section{Completing the leaves}
Only edges between opposite leaf sets remain undecided. At each partial
graph choose a deficient leaf $v$, requiring $s$ more neighbours. An
individual candidate is an opposite deficient leaf at distance at least
five from $v$, with disconnected pairs also allowed. If some deficient
leaf has fewer candidates than its deficit, the graph cannot be completed.
Otherwise take every $s$-element candidate set whose members have pairwise
distance at least four in the current graph, and add all its edges to $v$.

These are exactly the permissible choices for that leaf. An individual
new edge creates a short cycle precisely when an old path of length at
most four joins its ends. A cycle using two new edges at $v$ consists of
those edges and an old path between their other ends; it is forbidden
precisely when that path has length at most three. A simple cycle uses
at most two edges at $v$, so no additional joint condition is missing.
Any completed graph has exactly one of the listed missing neighbour sets.

The certificate records every visited literal graph, its initial form,
and its parent. A separate verifier reconstructs every candidate set and
requires equality with the entire recorded child list. Each step strictly
adds edges. Induction on the number of missing edges proves that every
completion is represented at a full leaf of the search. No canonical
labelling program or graph-isomorphism routine is used in this certificate.

At a full graph, the same Moore edge argument counts the six-cycles through
each edge: they are exactly the edges between that edge's two depth-two
leaf sets. The verifier applies this count to every edge and keeps exactly
the graphs whose individual counts are all twenty-one. Every retained
graph is identified with the source example by a supplied permutation,
checked on all $30^2$ ordered vertex pairs.

\section{The resulting classification}
The complete counts are as follows. Local forms are counted after the
explicit branch and leaf permutations described above; full graphs and
qualifying outputs are labelled records, not counts of isomorphism classes.
\[
\begin{array}{c|r|r|r|r|r}
Y&\text{local candidates}&\text{valid forms}&\text{nodes}&\text{full}&\text{qualifying}\\\hline
2K_2&516&228&8499&688&48\\
P_3\sqcup K_1&728&414&435&0&0
\end{array}
\]
The verifier checks all $642$ initial forms, all $8934$ tree nodes and
all $8292$ parent-child links. It separately verifies the short cycles
in all $602$ rejected local candidates. All $48$ qualifying records
have explicit bijections to \nolinkurl{UntilOrder250_181}.

\begin{theorem}
There is exactly one isomorphism class of connected graphs of order
thirty, degree four and girth six with twenty-one six-cycles through each
edge. Its order is the minimum for these parameters.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The local reduction and the complete branching certificate prove that
there is at most one class. A qualifying terminal graph proves existence.
The acquired note \emph{Three vertices outside a Moore tree} excludes
order twenty-nine, together with the separately proved smaller-order cases.
It establishes the lower bound of thirty used here.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and verification.}
The exact source entry is Table~2, physical page~18, of J.~Goedgebeur and
J.~Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of edge-girth-regular graphs},
\nolinkurl{arXiv:2401.08271v2}, 25 June 2024. Its earlier lower bound of
twenty-nine is attributed to Drglin, Filipovski, Jajcay and Raiman,
reference~[8, Theorem~2.3]; its earlier example of order thirty is attributed
to Kiss, Miklavi\v{c} and Sz\H{o}nyi, reference~[15, Example~5.4].
We retain these priorities. Our contribution is the exterior reduction
and the complete independently checkable classification certificate.
The already acquired Moore-tree and lower-bound arguments are reused.
This is a finite computational proof with a written completeness argument;
it is not a Lean formalization or a newly settled open conjecture.
\end{document}
